WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.620
Now, we know that there's no way
we're going to be able to fit a tree

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:10.450
to a non-additive matrix, by definition
of what a non-additive matrix is. But

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.320
maybe we can construct a tree that is,
in a sense,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.714
the best approximate solution for
a given distance matrix.

00:00:16.714 --> 00:00:22.200
For example, consider
the non-additive distance matrix that

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.810
we encountered earlier in the talk, shown
below, along with the following tree.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:33.100
To measure how close this tree
is to the distance matrix,

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:38.280
we need to compare the entries in D,
i.e., upper case D,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.978
with the distance between
the corresponding leaves in the tree,

00:00:40.978 --> 00:00:44.860
i.e., lower case d.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.190
The matrices are symmetric, so

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:49.540
we only need to consider the entries
above the main diagonal, of course.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:51.580
Now for this example,

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:55.810
notice that the red values disagree
between the matrix and the tree.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:01:02.534
The distance from i to l, for example, is 3,
not 4, which is what the tree indicates.

00:01:02.534 --> 00:01:08.620
And the distance from j to l is 5,
not 4, which is what the tree indicates.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:13.255
So in general we can compute how well
a tree fits a distance matrix by computing

00:01:13.255 --> 00:01:17.464
the sum of squared errors between
the distance matrix, upper case D,

00:01:17.464 --> 00:01:22.200
and the distances between leaves in
the tree that we're given, lower case d.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:27.945
So to denote this sum of squared errors,
we used the term "Discrepancy(T, D)".

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.490
For this particular tree, there
are only two non-zero errors,

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:40.390
both of which are equal to 1, and
so the discrepancy is equal to 2.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:43.470
However, this is not the best we can do for
this tree.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.390
Why don't you take a moment to see if
you can find edge weights that provide

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:47.740
a smaller value of discrepancy?

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:54.360
Can you find maybe a provably best
assignment of edge weights for this tree?

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.730
In general, there is a polynomial
algorithm that will minimize the sum of

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:07.130
squared errors if we are given the
structure of the tree (T) in advance,

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:11.120
but in practice, we're not going to know
the structure of the tree in advance,

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.329
so we'll need to minimize the sum of squared
errors over all possible tree structures.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:21.030
The number of tree structures is
exponential in the number of the leaves in

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:26.260
the tree, and so unfortunately, this least
squares distance based phylogeny problem

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:28.170
is NP-complete, and so

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:33.999
it's not going to help us fit a tree
to a non-additive matrix unfortunately.

